QPA: draw windows at the device pixel ratio of a scaled desktop

On a desktop drawn at a higher density (air/OS, BScreen::DeviceScale()),
Qt windows were rendered at their logical size and enlarged by the window
system, which made every Qt application blurry at 125 to 175% and
smeared its subpixel text.

The screen and the windows now report that scale as their device pixel
ratio, so Qt renders at the frame buffer's density. Window geometry,
events and the rest stay in the window system's coordinates. The
backing store has that many pixels per coordinate unit (rounded up so it
covers the window wherever it is), follows ratio changes in beginPaint(),
and is drawn one to one with B_DEVICE_PIXEL_BITMAP. B_SCREEN_CHANGED is
passed on to Qt (screen geometry and device pixel ratio), so a change of
scale repaints the windows at the new one.

Text on a scaled desktop is anti-aliased in grey and hinted vertically
only, as the system does there. BScreen::DeviceScale() is looked up at
run time; without it the ratio stays 1.

